# Postman Collections

Apply when creating or editing files under `postman/`. Add new conventions to this rule as they appear.

## Naming

### Collections

- Name = resource only, **all lowercase**
- No product/service prefix, no em dashes, no Title Case
- **Filename** = `{resource}.postman_collection.json` (same resource name; no `golang-template-` or other service prefix)

```
❌ Golang Template — Posts
❌ Posts
❌ golang-template-posts.postman_collection.json
✅ posts
✅ posts.postman_collection.json
```

### Folders and requests

- **all lowercase** (spaces OK for readability)
- Prefer verb + resource; put clarifying context in parentheses

```
❌ Create Post (with title)
✅ create post
✅ get post (by uuid)
```

### Variables

- Always **snake_case**
- Never camelCase or PascalCase

```
❌ baseUrl, accessToken, adminEmail, postId
✅ base_url, access_token, admin_email, post_id
```

## Base URLs

- `base_url` = host + `/api/v1` (all versioned API routes)
- `root_url` = host only (probes: `/live`, `/ready`, `/health`)

```
base_url = http://localhost:8080/api/v1
root_url = http://localhost:8080

✅ {{base_url}}/posts
✅ {{root_url}}/live
❌ {{base_url}}/api/v1/posts
❌ {{base_url}}/live
```

## Request payloads — dynamic variables

Prefer Postman dynamic (`{{$random…}}`) vars for sample body fields that are not fixed by business rules.

Keep **static** when value is constrained (roles, enums, known seed credentials, passwords used for later login, flags like `is_active`).

Randomize freely: names, emails, usernames, free-text titles/content when uniqueness helps.

```json
{
  "title": "{{$randomLoremSentence}}",
  "content": "{{$randomLoremParagraph}}",
  "visibility": "public"
}
```

In scripts: `pm.variables.replaceIn('{{$randomFirstName}}')`.

## Living document

Append new Postman conventions here as the team agrees on them.
